ou or a plural subject. Be describes identity, location or a state.","examples":["I am near the exit.","The keys are on a hook."]},{"title":"Negatives and questions","explanation":"Place not after be. For a standard yes/no question, put be before the subject. Do not add do or does.","examples":["The door is not locked.","Are the seats free?"]},{"title":"Contractions","explanation":"In everyday English, I am becomes I’m and they are becomes they’re. Full forms remain correct. In positive short answers, keep the full verb.","examples":["Yes, we are.","No, it is not."]}],"tips":["Read the full context before choosing.","Check the explanation, including after a correct answer.","A passing practice session is useful feedback, not a language certificate."],"source":"seed","provenance":{"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","authorship":"Grammacho; generated offline with AI assistance","kind":"original-guidance-aligned","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending","referenceIds":["bc-be","pcc-esol"],"assessment":"Practice only; levels are approximate teaching labels, not validated CEFR assessment."}}'::jsonb j) payload
